# QuickCleverModel — example .env. Copy to .env and edit:
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
# .env is the one source of truth for the whole stack: the native macOS
# scripts, the Linux Docker stack, and setup-opencode.sh all read it.
# Everything below is optional except BONSAI_API_KEY. Defaults shown are
# what the scripts use when the variable is unset — uncomment to change.

# --- required ---------------------------------------------------------------

# API key for llama-server (and Open WebUI, which proxies it). Generate one:
#   printf 'BONSAI_API_KEY=bonsai-%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 20)" >> .env
BONSAI_API_KEY=bonsai-change-me

# --- where the server is (client side: setup-opencode.sh, bench.sh) ---------

# BONSAI_HOST is the address llama-server BINDS on this machine
# (start-server.sh: --host "$HOST"). It defaults to 0.0.0.0 so the Docker VM
# can reach it — which also means it is reachable from your whole LAN, and the
# API key above is the only thing standing in the way. On an untrusted network
# (café, office, shared wifi) set 127.0.0.1 to keep it loopback-only; nothing
# outside the Docker bridge needs more.
#
# Do NOT put another machine's IP here. This is a bind address: a host cannot
# bind an address it does not own, and llama-server will fail to start with
# "Can't assign requested address".
#BONSAI_HOST=0.0.0.0
#BONSAI_PORT=8080

# Where setup-opencode.sh points opencode. NOT yet honoured by bench.sh, which
# still hardcodes 127.0.0.1 and reads BONSAI_API_KEY directly — so benchmarking
# a remote server is not configurable from here. Separate from BONSAI_HOST above because
# connect-address and bind-address are different facts: this one may name
# another machine — a Tailscale IP, say — when the server runs elsewhere.
# Defaults to this machine.
#BONSAI_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1

# API key for the server BONSAI_SERVER_URL points at. Defaults to
# BONSAI_API_KEY, which is correct when that server is this machine. Set it
# only when pointing at another box, whose key is its own — sending the local
# key to a remote server just yields 401.
#BONSAI_SERVER_KEY=

# --- publishing (Linux Docker path) -----------------------------------------

# Which host IPs the ports listen on. Loopback-only by default; set to a
# Tailscale IP to serve a tailnet. NEVER 0.0.0.0 unless you mean to share
# with the whole LAN — the UI has no login unless WEBUI_AUTH is flipped in
# docker/compose.linux.yaml. The API always requires the key.
#LLAMA_BIND=127.0.0.1
#WEBUI_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Second interface, e.g. a second tailscaled on another tailnet.
#LLAMA_BIND2=127.0.0.2
#WEBUI_BIND2=127.0.0.2
# Open WebUI port on the native macOS path (start-webui.sh).
#WEBUI_PORT=9090

# --- context and memory ------------------------------------------------------

# Context size in tokens. Model trains to 262144. Default 32768 on macOS,
# 8192 in docker/compose.linux.yaml (sized for 8 GB VRAM with KV on GPU).
# Must match between server .env and client .env (opencode compacts at it).
#BONSAI_CTX=8192

# true = KV cache in system RAM, weights stay on GPU. For boxes with little
# VRAM and plenty of RAM (RTX 3070 Ti 8 GB + 64 GB). Slower decode: KV is
# read over PCIe every token.
#BONSAI_NO_KV_OFFLOAD=false

# KV cache quant type. q4_0 is measured-best on Apple Metal (do not change
# for macOS). With host-side KV on Linux, q8_0 can beat q4_0 — the CPU KV
# fast path is gated on non-q4 types.
#BONSAI_CTK=q4_0
#BONSAI_CTV=q4_0

# Micro-batch size; sizes the peak compute buffer. 256 fixes cudaMalloc OOM
# on 8 GB cards at ~25% prefill cost. 512 is the upstream default.
#BONSAI_UBATCH=512

# Prompt cache in RAM, MiB. A 30k-token entry is ~3 GB on this model, so
# raise this for long agent sessions on big-RAM boxes.
#BONSAI_CACHE_RAM=2048
# Prompt-prefix reuse granularity in tokens.
#BONSAI_CACHE_REUSE=256

# How many models stay loaded at once (router mode). 1 = swap on selection,
# 2 = both hot (instant switching, needs the RAM/VRAM).
#BONSAI_MODELS_MAX=1
# Parallel request slots per model.
#BONSAI_PARALLEL=1

# --- ./update.sh (Linux/Docker only) -----------------------------------------
# These matter because update.sh rolls the stack back when it decides a model
# is broken, and pins the commit when the code is to blame. A deadline that is
# too short on a slow box therefore blacklists good code until someone clears
# it by hand, so raise these rather than let that happen.

# Seconds to wait for one model to answer the smoke test. Generous by design:
# /health answering says nothing about a loaded model, and in router mode the
# first request pays the whole cold load.
#BONSAI_SMOKE_TIMEOUT=900

# Ceiling on ONE whole smoke sweep, across every alias and its retries.
# Defaults to 4 x BONSAI_SMOKE_TIMEOUT — 3x leaves no headroom for the
# /v1/models call or any backoff when three aliases are configured, so the
# last one gets skipped. The systemd unit's time budget is written against
# this, so raising it means raising TimeoutStartSec too.
#BONSAI_SMOKE_SWEEP_MAX=3600

# Seconds to wait for llama's /health after a restart. Blowing this rolls back
# AND pins the commit, so it matches install.sh's own budget for the same
# endpoint (900s, "model load on first request is genuinely slow") rather than
# risking a blacklisted commit on a slow box.
#BONSAI_HEALTH_TIMEOUT=900

# Seconds to wait for the Open WebUI chat interface. Separate from the above
# and longer on purpose: its first start after a :main pull runs database
# migrations. Blowing this rolls back an otherwise-good update.
#BONSAI_WEBUI_TIMEOUT=300

# Backoff between retries when the router says it is busy, multiplied by the
# attempt number. 0 retries immediately.
#BONSAI_SMOKE_RETRY_SLEEP=15

# --- generation --------------------------------------------------------------

# Cap on thinking tokens. The think block is closed cleanly when the budget
# runs out. -1 = unlimited, 0 = end thinking at once.
#BONSAI_REASONING_BUDGET=4096

# Speculative decoding. ngram-simple is measured-best on Metal; ngram-mod
# wins on repetitive output (code, tool-call JSON). The three ngram-mod
# tuning flags only apply when BONSAI_SPEC=ngram-mod.
#BONSAI_SPEC=ngram-simple
#BONSAI_NGRAM_MOD_N_MATCH=24
#BONSAI_NGRAM_MOD_N_MIN=48
#BONSAI_NGRAM_MOD_N_MAX=64

# --- Hetzner Inference API (optional, hosted models) -------------------------

# Adds Hetzner's hosted open-weight models to the SAME Open WebUI dropdown as
# the local Bonsai models. Leave unset (the default) and the stack stays
# entirely local — nothing is sent anywhere.
#
# PRIVACY: setting this sends your prompts off this machine to Hetzner. Every
# other part of this stack runs locally. Hetzner state they keep usage metadata
# (timestamps, token counts) and NOT request or response content — their
# infrastructure, their claim. Decide accordingly.
#
# Models available during their experimental phase (query /v1/models for the
# definitive list — it changes):
#   Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-FP8   MoE 35B total / 3B active, 262144 ctx, text+image
#   Qwen3.8-27B                dense, 262144 ctx, text+image
#
# RATE LIMITS, per key: 10 requests / 60s, 4M input and 100k output tokens / 60s.
# Exceeding any of them returns HTTP 429. The 10 req/min ceiling is low for a
# chat UI — this stack already disables Open WebUI's title/tag/follow-up/
# autocomplete generation (see start-webui.sh), each of which would otherwise
# fire extra requests per message and burn that budget quickly.
#
# Free while the API is in experimental status.
#HETZNER_API_KEY=
#HETZNER_API_BASE=https://inference.hetzner.com/api/v1

# --- Playwright MCP (start-playwright-mcp.sh) --------------------------------

#PW_MCP_PORT=8931
#PW_MCP_BROWSER=chrome
# none = do not attach the page tree to every tool response (huge); the
# model can call browser_snapshot when it needs it.
#PW_MCP_SNAPSHOT=none
# Cap on the total bytes in PW_MCP_OUTPUT_DIR. Once exceeded, the oldest
# artifacts are DELETED. Default 536870912 (512 MiB); set 0 to keep everything
# forever (the directory then grows without bound).
#PW_MCP_OUTPUT_MAX_SIZE=536870912

# Where screenshots and other files the browser tools write are saved.
# Defaults to <repo>/.playwright-mcp, which is gitignored and dockerignored.
# Use an ABSOLUTE path: a relative one resolves against the working directory
# the service was launched from, not the repo, so dumps can land outside the
# ignore rules and end up committed or in the Docker build context.
#PW_MCP_OUTPUT_DIR=/absolute/path/to/playwright-output
